Part 6 Phase 0: infrastructure audit and implementation plan

Recorded before any code is modified, per step 18 of the Part 6 brief.

Audit findings
- Cache: the CacheProvider interface and the narrow getOrSet/peek/invalidate
  facade already exist in services/cache.service.js, so Redis becomes a
  provider implementation rather than a rewrite. Missing: provider selection,
  the Upstash provider, key namespacing, versioning, and a registry that
  records owner, invalidation trigger and privacy per entry.
- Rate limiting: two independent process-local mechanisms need a shared
  counter backend - express-rate-limit for the 21 HTTP domains, and the
  SlidingWindow in utils/frequency-limiter.js for the action and socket
  guards. The 429 contract itself is complete and must be preserved verbatim.
- Idempotency: the middleware is process-local AND mounted on only two routes.
  Ticket issuance, quiz submissions and event submissions are unprotected
  today, which the brief explicitly calls out.
- Identity: canonical identity is the MongoDB users ObjectId carried in a
  JWT. Supabase Auth is not used and must not be introduced. Because an
  ObjectId is not a Postgres uuid, relational rows will reference a
  deterministic UUIDv5 derived from the ObjectId, so the mapping needs no
  lookup table and no stored state.
- Realtime: all room, participant and cap state lives in process-local Maps.
  This is precisely why the Socket.IO Redis adapter cannot simply be enabled -
  the adapter shares pub/sub but not that registry. Part 6 ships the
  abstraction and a readiness checklist instead.
- Social models: the existing Mongo unique and composite indexes map directly
  onto Postgres constraints and are enumerated in the plan so nothing is lost
  in translation.

Decisions recorded with reasons
- Key format eh:v1:cache:event:{id} combines the brief's cache-prefix and
  version requirements into one string, so bumping v1 to v2 invalidates
  everything via a single env change.
- eventInterest stays in MongoDB even though the brief lists it under
  Supabase: it is a (event, user) edge between the Mongo event core and a
  user, so splitting it buys nothing and costs a cross-database relationship.
- Consistency uses a Mongo-side outbox with retry-safe consumers. No
  distributed transactions, and Mongo stays authoritative so projections are
  always replayable.

Nine implementation phases follow, each with its own selftest and a full
Part 5 regression run before commit.
